      "message": "\u003e \u003e Please look through all this test file. It doesn\u0027t test or verify anything.\n\u003e \n\u003e Yes I agree with this, this is why this is a nitpick.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e It\u0027s just making calls to generate code coverage, and I\u0027m trying to fix it.\n\u003e \n\u003e I don\u0027t think this is the right way to generate code coverage, code coverage is not a very good metric mostly due to being able to generate some with test like that which doesn\u0027t carry a lot of meaning.\n\u003e \n\u003e The only value that I could see to such test is to please management that will see this metric increase (and even that might not happen because this function might be already covered, did you check and measured ?).\n\nI wrote that these tests just generate code coverage and I want to fix it -  I want them to actually be valuable. I don\u0027t care about actual code coverage %.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e Can you please recommend when such test would not be a change detector ?\n\u003e \n\u003e I think a significant part of our tests are change detectors, to write a good test you usually need to have a good testable api ... which we don\u0027t really have in my opinion.\n\u003e \n\u003e https://testing.googleblog.com/2013/08/testing-on-toilet-test-behavior-not.html\n\u003e https://testing.googleblog.com/2015/01/testing-on-toilet-prefer-testing-public.html\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e What would I have to fix in it ? It just seems like every unit test would fall into this category.\n\u003e \n\u003e I would say that you should enlarge the scope of the test (test the behavior of bta_dm_sdp_find_services and down the call chain)  to exercice some kind of logic, like for example checking that calling this function sends a packet over SDP\n\nIf I enlarge the scope of the test, it\u0027s no longer unit test, it\u0027s integration test. Going this way (exagerrated), we would have just tests of whole system.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e tests also serve as documentation and act as a safety net for refactoring\n\u003e \n\u003e True but what does this test documents ? And what would it catch in case of refactoring?\n\u003e \n\u003e For me the answer is nothing for both questions.\n\nThis test documents that when Classic service discovery is requested from bta_dm_disc.cc, we would call SDP API, and then, once SDP API calls back to  proper callback would be triggered once this API calls back into bta_dm_disc.cc\nIMHO this is not trivial, or obvious from looking at this code.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e Article you mentioned doesn\u0027t provide any positive example,\n\u003e \n\u003e I don\u0027t know any rule on how to write a good test, it mostly depends on the context and what we want to test, here I don\u0027t really understand what do we want to test (what behavior).\n\nI think this is matter of personal preference, and we could have an never ending argument here.\n\nFolowing the articles you mentioned, no unit test would pass a bar of good test.\n\nIf you folow Test Driven Development principles, i.e. as in https://g3doc.corp.google.com/codelab/unittesting/cpp/g3doc/index.md?cl\u003dhead , then you end up with every test being \"change detector\".\n\nBut then, some group of engineers would say TDD is the best way to write code and tests.",
